1. How is Shell Scripting used in the industry?

=> As a Linux/System Administrator or DevOps Engineer, you can use Shell scripts for many things.

a) Example 1: Disk space checking

You can create a script that checks:

If / is above 80%
        ↓
Send an alert

=> For example:

Disk usage = 85%

Script:
"WARNING: Disk usage is above 80%"

b) Example 2: Backup

Suppose you need to backup a directory every night.

Instead of manually doing:

tar -czf backup.tar.gz /data

=> you create a script.

Then schedule it using cron.

Every night at 11 PM
        ↓
Cron
        ↓
Backup script
        ↓
Backup created

c) Example 3: Log cleanup

Suppose old log files are taking too much space.

A script can find old files and remove them.

=> Find logs older than 30 days
        ↓
Delete them
        ↓
Free disk space

d) Example 4: Service checking

You can create a script to check whether a service is running.

systemctl is-active nginx

=> If nginx is down:

Nginx is down
      ↓
Start nginx
      ↓
Send alert

e) Example 5: User creation

Suppose you need to create 20 Linux users.

Instead of manually running:

useradd user1
useradd user2
useradd user3
...

you can write a script that creates all users automatically.

2. Shell scripting in your Linux Support job

Since you are working toward Linux System Administration, Shell Scripting is useful because many Linux admin tasks can be automated.

=> For example, your daily work might involve:

Check server health
      ↓
Check disk
      ↓
Check memory
      ↓
Check services
      ↓
Check logs
      ↓
Restart service if required

You can gradually automate these tasks with Shell scripts.

3. Shell Scripting vs Ansible

This is also important for you because you're learning Ansible.

Think of it this way:

a) Shell Script

Usually good for automating tasks on one machine or tasks where command-line logic is enough.

Linux Server
     ↑
Shell Script

b) Ansible

Designed to automate and manage multiple servers.

             Ansible
           /    |    \
          ↓     ↓     ↓
       Server1 Server2 Server3

=> For example:

a) Shell scripting:

apt update
apt install nginx
systemctl start nginx

b) Ansible:

You can describe the desired state:

- name: Install nginx
  apt:
    name: nginx
    state: present

Ansible then manages the server for you.

4. One simple real-world example

Imagine you are a Linux administrator.

Your manager says:

"Every morning, check all servers and tell me if any server has low disk space."

=> Manually:

Login to server 1
↓
df -h

Login to server 2
↓
df -h

Login to server 3
↓
df -h

...

This is repetitive.

=> With automation:

Shell Script
     ↓
Check disk space
     ↓
If usage > 80%
     ↓
Show WARNING

And later you could use Ansible to run that check across many servers.
